<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dong Zhongshu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/179-bce">179</a>–104 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Tung Chung-shu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dong Zhongshu was a Chinese philosopher, politician and writer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, traditionally associated with the promotion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> as the official ideology of the imperial state. Born in modern Hengshui, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, he rose to high office under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a>, though he was once imprisoned and nearly executed for writings considered seditious. His thought integrated Yin Yang cosmology into a Confucian ethical framework, and he is considered the originator of the doctrine of Interactions Between Heaven and Mankind, which set rules for the legitimacy of a monarch. The Luxuriant Dew of the Spring and Autumn Annals is attributed to him, though scholars doubt much of it is his.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dong-zhongshu.jpg" alt="Dong Zhongshu" width="330" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:歷代聖賢半身像_冊_董仲舒_(Dong_Zhongshu).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
